---
import type { IconName } from "astro-icon";
import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import Base from "../layouts/base.astro";

// The user's saved choices, as they'd come back from a database: arbitrary names
// from anywhere in the mdi pack, not a curated shortlist.
const SAVED = [
  "rocket-launch",
  "penguin",
  "coffee",
  "guitar-electric",
  "weather-lightning",
  "cactus",
  "sail-boat",
  "ufo",
];
---

<Base title="Catalog picker">
  <h1>Icon names from an open catalog</h1>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>UC4, the harder half.</strong> An end user picking from the full catalog
    can land on any of 7,000+ <code>mdi</code> icons, so the
    <code>catalog</code> collection has no <code>allowed: [...]</code> allowlist -
    every sync loads and types the entire pack instead of a curated slice.
  </p>

  <div class="grid">
    {
      SAVED.map((name) => (
        <div class="cell">
          {/*
          The cast is unavoidable here, and it's the real cost of an open catalog.
          `IconName` is a union of concrete names, so a string assembled at runtime
          can never satisfy it. On the nav page the same data-driven pattern still
          type-checks, because its set is small enough to write down; once the set
          is "any of 7,000", a typo stops being a compile error and becomes a
          missing icon at request time.
        */}
          <Icon name={`catalog:${name}` as IconName} size={28} />
          <code>{name}</code>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    It also costs you the type check. <code>IconName</code> is a union of concrete
    names, so a string built at runtime can't satisfy it and this page needs a cast
    - a typo here surfaces as a missing icon on a request, not as a build failure.
    Compare with <a href="/">the CMS-driven nav</a>: same "names from data"
    problem, opposite right answer on both counts, decided entirely by whether
    the set of possible names is small enough to write down.
  </p>
</Base>
